## The Idea

It started when I just wanted to play old Nintendo games on my phone. I went looking for an emulator, and what I found was overwhelming: apps full of ads that charged you just to save your game. You couldn't really enjoy playing that way.

So I decided to make the one I wished existed, my own mini console in my pocket.

- **Inspired by the Classics:** A love letter to the Game Boy Color and Nintendo consoles.
- **Free of Ads:** Just you and the game, the way it used to be.
- **Beautiful Skins:** The only thing for sale is optional skins, because a console should look like yours.

## Designing the Feeling of a Real Console

I didn't want to make an emulator. I wanted to bring back what it felt like to hold one of these consoles, so I designed around those small rituals.

### A Gallery of Cartridges

I designed the game library as a shelf of cartridges instead of a list of files, sorted by console. Browsing your games feels like looking through your collection.

### Insert It, Hear the Click

You don't tap a game to open it. You put the cartridge into the console, hear the click, and watch it power on with a startup screen, just like you used to.

### Buttons You Can Feel

The on-screen buttons vibrate when you press them, so your thumbs get some of that feedback back. It's a tiny detail, and it's what makes it feel like a console and not just a screen.

![Three RetroPoket Color screens: the play screen with physical-feeling buttons, Bluetooth controller remapping, and the skin store](/images/projects/retropoket-play-settings-store.webp)

## Building It With Claude Code

This is where my workflow moved past the Figma file. I built the app with Claude Code, working from my own UI designs.

- **Three Consoles in One:** The app plays Pocket (.gb, .gbc), Pocket Advance (.gba) and 16-bit Console (.sfc, .smc) games, with the emulators bundled inside.
- **Bluetooth Controllers:** Players can pair a controller and remap any button. I tested it with a Luna controller.
- **A Fair Business Model:** The app is free, and optional skins are a one-time purchase.
- **No Games Included:** People play copies of cartridges they own, or free homebrew games from indie developers.

## Where It Is Now

- **Android:** Already on Google Play, in its user testing period with a group of real testers.
- **iPhone:** A free web version runs in Safari today, and a proper iOS app is planned for the future.
- **Website:** A product page at anitools.dev that explains the app and invites new testers.
